:chedit_chno
      [Channel Number for Display on the Transceiver]

 Pressing the Space Bar, toggles the data for the entire
 channel between hidden and unhidden (except the first
 channel, which cannot be hidden).
 Hidden channels display "--" in place of field entries, and
 are not used for operation (although they are still stored
 in hidden form for possible recall later).


 Space Bar:  Hide/Unhide entire channel (line).

#
#
#
:chedit_rxf
   [Edit Rx/Simplex Frequency & Hide/Unhide Channel Data]

 Use 0 - 9 to enter the desired channel frequency, and press
 Enter. This frequency will be adjusted if it does not match
 the Channel Step parameter, and will also appear in the Tx
 Freq field. You do not need to enter all 8 digits: empty
 digits to the right are zero-filled. Pressing [.] (period)
 after several digits forces them to MHz. Press period first
 to change only kHz.

 Pressing only the Space Bar without entering a number
 toggles the data for the entire channel between hidden and
 unhidden (except the first channel, which cannot be hidden).
 Hidden channels display "--" in place of field entries, and
 are not used for operation (although they are still stored
 in hidden form for possible recall later).
#
#
#
:page
#

 NOTE! Any entries or changes to this field will also occur
       in the Tx Freq. field, so you may need to edit that
       field after making any changes here.


 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                Frequency).
 Space Bar:  Hide/Unhide entire channel (line).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.

#
#
#
:chedit_txf
  [Enter Transmit Frequency & Enable/Disable Transmission]

 Use keys 0 - 9 to enter the desired transmitting frequency,
 and press the Enter key to accept. If the frequency does not
 match the Channel Step parameter, it will be adjusted to the
 nearest valid step. You do not need to enter all 8 digits:
 empty digits to the right are zero-filled.
 Pressing [.] (period) after several digits forces them to
 MHz, while pressing [.] first allows entering only kHz.
 
 Pressing only the Space Bar without entering a number
 toggles the the data for the entire channel between hidden
 and unhidden(except the first channel, which cannot be 
 hidden). Hidden channels display "--" in place of field
 entries, and are not used for operation (although they are
 still stored in hidden form for possible recall later).
#
#
#
:page
#

 NOTE! The Tx Freq is automatically copied from the Rx Freq,
      so any changes here will be LOST if the Rx Freq is
      changed. Therefore, the Rx Freq should be set first,
      before editing the Tx Freq. 

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after Editing Freq.)
 Space Bar:  Hide/Unhide entire channel (line).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits to MHz, or
               next digits to kHz.
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:chedit_cdec
 [Toggle Subaudible CTCSS Decoder, and set CTCSS frequency]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the decoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Tone Select window, from which you may
 select a frequency with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the CTCSS frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys. When the
 desired CTCSS frequency is displayed, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones),
 the Tone Select Window will appear as described above, with
 the nearest valid frequency pre-selected.
#
:page
#

 Turning on CTCSS turns off DCS (both cannot be on).
 CTCSS cannot be selected for use if ARTS is enabled (DCS 
 only).

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
#
:chedit_ddec
          [Toggle DCS Decoder, and set DCS Code]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the decoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Code Select window, from which you may
 select a DCS Code with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the 3-digit DCS Code you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 keys. When the desired Code is
 displayed, press Enter to accept. If the Code was invalid
 (not among the standard Code numbers), the Code Select
 window will appear as described above, with the nearest
 valid Code pre-selected.
#
:page
#

 Turning on DCS turns off CTCSS (both cannot be on).
 DCS encode and decode are both required for ARTS operation.

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 7:      Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_cenc
   [Toggle Tone Squelch Encoder, and set CTCSS frequency]

 Press Space to toggle the Encoder on/off, or press Enter to
 display the Tone Select window (if not viewing it already),
 from which you may select a frequency with the Arrow Keys
 (pressing Enter again to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the CTCSS frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys (entering
 a number or [.] forces the Encoder on). When the display
 shows the desired CTCSS frequency, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones)
 the Tone Select Window will appear as mentioned above.

 NOTE! if the channel is hidden when you make an entry, it
 will be unhidden. Edit the Rx Freq field to hide it again.
#
#
#
:page
#

 NOTE: Turning on the CTCSS Encoder automatically turns off
 the DCS encoder, if it was on.
 
 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_denc
       [Toggle DCS Encoder, and set DCS Encoder Code]

 Press Space to toggle the Encoder on/off, or press Enter to
 display the Code Select window (if not viewing it already),
 from which you may select a code with the Arrow Keys
 (pressing Enter again to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the DCS code you want, you can enter it directly
 with the 0 - 7 keys (entering a number forces the Encoder
 on). When the display shows the desired code, press Enter to
 accept. If the code was invalid (not among the standard
 codes) the Code Select Window will appear as mentioned
 above.

 NOTE! if the channel is hidden when you make an entry, it
 will be unhidden. Edit the Rx Freq field to hide it again.
#
#
#
:page
#

 NOTE: Turning on the DCS Encoder automatically turns off
 the CTCSS encoder, if it was on.
 
 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 7:      Direct code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
                selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_artsmode
                        [ARTS Mode]

 ARTS (Automatic Range Transpond System) handshakes 
 periodically with the user's ARTS-compatible portable radio.
 If the portable fails to handshake with the VXR-1000 in two
 successive handshake cycles, the PWR lamp begins blinking to
 indicate out-of-range status.
 The PWR lamp will return to steady-on once ARTS re-
 establishes contact with the portable.
#
:page
#
 ARTS operates in one of four modes, described as follows:
 
 - OFF:	ARTS is not active on this channel.  No handshaking
   is performed.
 - RX:	The VXR-1000 receives handshake signals from the
   portable, but does not send them.  If an out-of-range
   condition occurs, the VXR-1000 will indicate out-of-range
   locally, but the portable user will NOT receive an 
   out-of-range indication.
 - TX:	The VXR-1000 sends handshake signals, but does not
   receive them.  If an out-of-range condition occurs, the
   portable user will receive an out-of-range indication
   (if the portable is properly programmed), but the VXR-1000
    will NOT indicate an out-of-range condition locally.
 - TRX:	Both the RX and TX functions described above are 
   active.
#
:page
#

 ARTS requires DCS encode and decode for operation.  It will
 not operate in CTCSS or carrier squelch.  The portable 
 radio's ARTS mode must be in complement in order for ARTS
 to function correctly.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields (after pressing Enter).
 Space Bar:  Toggle ARTS mode.
#
#
#
:chedit_artsint
                    [ARTS Tx Interval]

 The ARTS TX interval is the amount of time between ARTS 
 handshakes transmitted by the VXR-1000.  There are two 
 selections, 25 or 55 seconds.  The portable radio's ARTS 
 interval must match this in order for ARTS to function 
 correctly.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle ARTS Tx Interval 25sec/55sec.
#
#
#
:chedit_dcstype
                       [DCS Type]

 Effective only when DCS is chosen as the coded squelch.
 A = Normal DCS
 B = Inverted (complement) DCS

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle A/B.
#
#
#
:chedit_ddectype
                      [DCS Dec Type]
  
 Selects ability to decode the DCS complement.
 Fixed = Decodes the selected type only (normal ORinverted)
 Auto  = Decodes both types (normal AND inverted)

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Auto/Fixed.
#
#
#
:chedit_sqlofst
                     [Squelch Offset]

 Provides a means to fine tune the VXR-1000 squelch level on
 a per-channel basis without having to perform a full 
 alignment.

 Enter digital proportional values (0 to 255).  Squelch
 threshold increases as the number entered increases.

 Arrow Keys:          Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar/Backspace: Increment/Decrement Squelch Offset 
                      Level.
 0 - 9:               Input a digit.
#
:page
#

 NOTE:  Although Squelch Offset range is between 0 and 255,
 however, squelch threshold will be close to saturation if
 the value is set to 100 or above. Therefore there will be
 no significant difference in squelch threshold between the
 value of 100 and 255. 
#
#
#
:chedit_encrypt
                       [Encryption]

 When ON, the VXR-1000 operates in the encrypted mode on this
 channel.
 This encryption type is compatible with the encryption used
 in the Vertex VX-10 (FTT-15), VX-200 (FSU-1083), 
 VX-400 (FVP-25), VX-500 and -510 (FVP-22).

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_wide
                   [Wide/Narrow toggle]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the FM mode Wide or Narrow.
 Wide = 25 kHz channel spacing, 5 kHz deviation.
 Narrow = 12.5 kHz channel spacing, 2.5 kHz deviation.
 
 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle wide/narrow.
 
#
#
#
:chedit_mod
                        [MOD Input]

 Selects how the VXR-1000 will manage audio signals being
 input for transmission to the associated portable radio.

 If MIX is selected, any audio being transmitted by the
 microphone on the connected mobile radio will also be
 transmitted to the portable on the other band.  If a second
 microphone is connected to the VXR-1000 and is also keyed
 at the same time, the audio from both microphones will be
 mixed and transmitted to the portable.
#
:page
#

 If MONO is selected, the VXR-1000 will only select audio
 from the microphone that first keyed it's PTT.  Audio from
 the other microphone, if it is keyed while the first 
 microphone remains keyed, will be ignored.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting MOD Inuput.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Mix/Mono.
#
#
#
:chedit_mblmon
                      [MBL Monitor]

 Selects whether or not the receive audio from the connected
 mobile radio will be routed to the VXR-1000's external
 speaker jack.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_txpwrlv
                     [Transmit Power]

 Selects TX power settings for this channel. 
 Choices are 0.5, 1.0, 2.5, and 5.0 watts.

 Arrow Keys:          Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar/Backspace: Increment/Decrement Transmit Power Level.

#
#
#
:chedit_trunk
                        [Trunking]

 Selects intercommunication protocol with the connected 
 mobile radio.  

 If the mobile radio is a conventional two-way radio, 
 choose OFF.  

 If the mobile radio is a trunked radio (SmarTrunk, LTR, 
 etc.), choose ON.  Also, set the Trunking Data parameters
 under Common Data as appropriate for operation with the 
 mobile radio.  Consult the Service Manuals for the VXR-1000
 and the trunked mobile radio to determine proper settings. 
#
:page
#
 
 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_cblip
                      [Courtesy Blip]

 When ON, the VXR-1000 sends out a beep on the portable
 radio frequency each time the portable radio is unkeyed.
 This beep is not tranmsitted by the connected mobile radio. 
 
 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_mblmic
                        [MBL Mic]

 Selects whether the audio from the mobile radio's 
 microphone will be transmitted by the VXR-1000 to the
 portable radio.

 If ON, audio from the mobile radio's microphone will be
 transmitted by both the mobile radio and the VXR-1000.

 If OFF, audio from the mobile radio's microphone will only
 be transmitted by the mobile radio.  

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_micctrl
                       [Mic Control]

 Selects which radio will transmit audio from the microphone
 connected to the VXR-1000.

 If INT, it will only transmit on the VXR-1000 to the 
 portable radio.  It will not transmit on the connected 
 mobile radio.

 If EXT, it will only transmit on the connected mobile radio.
 It will not tranmsit on the VXR-1000 to the portable radio.

 If BOTH, both radios will transmit.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting Mic Control.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Internal/Extenal/Both.
#
#
#
:chedit_extctrl
                   [Ext. Radio Control]
                    
 Selects how the VXR-1000 will control and be controlled by
 the connected mobile radio.

 When ON, signals received by the mobile radio will be re-
 transmitted to the portable radio AND signals from the 
 portable radio will be re-transmitted by the mobile radio.

 When RX, signals received by the mobile radio will be re-
 transmitted to the portable radio, but signals from the 
 portable radio will NOT be re-transmitted by the mobile 
 radio.
#
:page
#

 When OFF, signals will not be re-transmitted by either 
 radio in either direction.

 In all cases, local receive audio is still active in the
 radio receiving a signal.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields (after pressing Enter).
 Space Bar:  Toggle Ext. Radio Control mode.
# Space Bar, Backspace: Toggle *** mode.
#
#
#
:chedit_pritmr
                     [Priority Timer]

 Effective when set to a value greater than 0.00 and when
 the VXR-1000 is transmitting to the poprtable radio. 
 This is the interval at which the VXR-1000 will momentarily
 unkey it's transmitter and listen for a possible
 transmission from the portable radio.  If a transmission
 from the portable is detected, the VXR-1000 will cease
 transmitting to the portable and will key up the connected
 mobile radio to transmit to the "base".  This allows the
 portable radio user to override an incoming transmission
 from the "base" if desired.

 Settings are 0.25 seconds thru 3.00 seconds.  0.00 = off.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change fields (after pressing Enter).
 Space Bar:  Incriment Priority Timer.
 Backspace:  Decriment Priority Timer.
#
#
#
:chedit_mtone
                       [Master Tone]

 When Lktn is chosen in Master/Slave Mode, user has the
 choices of 847.5Hz or 832.5Hz for the desired Master Tone.

 Arrow Keys: Change field after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Master Tone 847.5Hz/832.5Hz.
#
#
#
:chedit_mstslv
                      [Master/Slave]

 Determines how this VXR-1000 will behave when it is in-
 range of others operating on the same frequency, such as
 when several mobile units are at a common location.  One
 VXR-1000 must act as the Master to provide portable-to-base
 relay for all portable users at the location.  All other
 VXR-1000s that are present must revert to Slave(inactive)
 status or they will cause a cacaphony of interference with
 each other.
#
:page
#

 Ordinarily, the first VXR-1000 arriving at a location will
 establish itself as the Master by periodically transmitting
 a Master signal tone.  Any other VXR-1000 coming within
 range will receive this tone and revert itself to Slave
 status.  If the Master happens to leave the location, the
 absence of it's Master signal tone will be detected by the
 other Slaves.  The first Slave detecting the absence of the
 Master will then transmit the Master signal tone.  If
 unchallenged by any other VXR-1000 in range, this Slave
 will then become the new Master for this location.  All of
 these transactions occur automatically and without
 interruption of voice communications occurring through the
 VXR-1000 system.  

 Select between the YAESU system (a Yaesu exclusive system
 based on sub-audible DCS signalling) or the Lock Tone
 system (employs audible tone bursts for signalling).
#
:page
#

 Important note:  If the YAESU system is used, you MUST
 program only CTCSS or carrier squelch to be used for the
 main coded squelch signal (items immediately to the left of
 the operating frequencies).  The CE-22 software will not
 allow use of DCS coded squelch if the YAESU system is
 selected.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields (after pressing Enter).
 Space Bar:  Toggle Master/Slave mode.
#
#
#
:chedit_msdcs
                 [Master/Slave DCS code]

 Selects the DCS code to employ for the YAESU Master/Slave
 signalling system.

 Important note:  If the YAESU system is used, you MUST
 program only CTCSS or carrier squelch to be used for the
 main coded squelch signal (items immediately to the left of
 the operating frequencies).  The CE-22 software will not
 allow use of DCS coded squelch if the YAESU system is
 selected.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 0 - 7:      Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.

